    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338-a2">

                <pb facs="0633" n="563" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg563"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Collector Sullivan to Mr. Folger.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Custom-House, San Francisco, Cal.</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Collector’s Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-04-19"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 19,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: A letter has been received at this office
                    from the United States consul at Hong-Kong advising of the shipment of several
                    cargoes of sugar from that port to Honolulu, Sandwich Islands, and expressing a
                    fear that it may be intended to transship said sugar there for the United States
                    and attempt to obtain free entry thereof at this port as the product of the
                    Hawaiian Islands.</p>
                <p>I have taken proper precautions to prevent any such possible attempt from “being
                    successful, and have forwarded a copy of Consul Mosby’s letter to the United
                    States consul at Honolulu.</p>
                <p>The Department is thus advised in order that instructions may be sent to the
                    latter by the Department of State if deemed necessary.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>C. L. SULLIVAN,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Collector.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
